A||||9||falconhulk||3rd brakelight project||||1||
Z||000000||falconhulk||11-05-2001||01:57 PM||||I am working on a 3rd brake light that I want to mount on the inside of the rear window.  I want to use ultrabright leds that i will flash in pattens when the brake is pushed.  I am having a brain lock on what to use as an enclosure for the leds.  Any ideas?  Something as wide as the rearwindow plastic and black or clear would do.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||MinnGreenGT||11-05-2001||03:05 PM||||You could use the same light lens that I used on my clear taillights...? It's technically clear, has a cool pattern, and transmits light well (texture toward the light source).<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GTfin03.jpg"> <P>It's a styrene hung-ceiling lens... about $4 at Home Depot for a 4'x8' sheet of it.  You can cut it by scoring the smooth side then just snapping it over a straight edge.  It can also be heated/flexed if necessary.||||reg||1
Z||000002||falconhulk||11-05-2001||03:22 PM||||MinnGreenGT<BR>Cool!  Thanks alot.  I was thinking plexiglass but nobody sells sheets of standard clear plexiglass.  That may be perfect.  I even called Homedepot and they said they had a cutting tool but no plexiglass sheets.  Why sell the tool and not the sheets?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||11-05-2001||03:33 PM||||You can buy textured plexiglass at hardware store. You can also use smooth and scratch up lite side with 80 grit sandpaper to defuse it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MinnGreenGT||11-05-2001||03:46 PM||||You can see that this diffuses the light <B>very</B> nicely & pretty evenly.  And these shots are with the LED bulb replacements (10 or 12 LEDs bundled together).<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GTfin01.jpg"> <P>Falcon... I'm curious about what you're coming up with, 'cause I had to disable my 3rd Brakelight to get the LEDs to work properly (the quartz bulb caused a bad ground).||||reg||1
Z||000005||falconhulk||11-05-2001||04:53 PM||||I Only have large hardware stores such as Lowes and Homedepot around here and For lowes i was on hold 15 minutes and HD said they only sold plexiglasscutter.  Not sure but I think i am going to go with clear plexiglass now that i think about it.  I am not sure if difused will provide the effect i want.  What I want to do is run a flash pattern when the brakes is hit for more then 3 seconds.  They will be on solid but after 3 seconds run a knightrider like movement and then a flash sequence.  The electronics are the easy part.  Just need to create the enclosure that will look good and be functional.  Also have to get LED's with a wide view dispersement so I achive the visual impact I want.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 11-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||sprnt96||11-05-2001||05:42 PM||||Do 84's have the mounting studs on the roof for a 3rd brake light?? I want to put on in but I don't want it to be a major project||||reg||1
Z||000007||MinnGreenGT||11-05-2001||06:00 PM||||I still would suggest some sort of a diffuser... a led by itself only lights a <B>very</B> small area.  With the diffuser, it "glows" and fills the area much better.  It'd sure look cool either way.  Can't wait to see what you've come up with!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||11-05-2001||08:22 PM||||Well, I guarantee you the Lowes will have plexiglass.  At the one here, they have a fair selection of it.  I can't believe you called the hardware store!!!  Half the fun is just walking around the hardware store coming up with ideas <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>If you want some acrylic, mcmastercarr.com is a great source.  They have about anything you'd ever need for a project, check them out.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||falconhulk||11-06-2001||08:25 AM||||Nashco, when you work 7:00am to 7:00pm not a whole lot of time to walk the HW store.  Especially when you are dreaming up projects at work!  lol!||||reg||1
